<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ellwangen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ellwangen (Jagst)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ellwangen is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagst">Jagst</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It grew around a Benedictine abbey founded around 764, converted in 1460 into a provostry of noble canons whose prince-provosts ruled the surrounding territory. The town was the scene of severe witch trials in the early 17th century. Annexed by Württemberg in 1802 and 1803, it keeps a Romanesque basilica, a castle and the pilgrimage church of Schönenberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ellwangen.jpg" alt="Ellwangen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moggosaurier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ellwangen_Stadtansicht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ellwangen lies in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagst">Jagst</a>, which runs through the town from south to north, between the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Alb</a> and the Virngrund forest, part of the Swabian-Franconian Forest. It is in the district of Ostalbkreis, about 17 kilometres north of Aalen. The Virngrund was known in antiquity as Virgundia, and the town developed in the 7th century as an Alemannic settlement in the forest next to the Franconian-Swabian border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 764 Hariolf, a Frankish noble and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>, founded the Benedictine Ellwangen Abbey on a hill beside the settlement. A document of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> named it Elehenuuwang in 814, and it became a Reichsabtei in 817. From 870 to 873 Saint Methodius, the Byzantine Greek &quot;Apostle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>&quot;, was held at Ellwangen after Ermanrich, bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, had arrested him; he was freed in 873 through the intervention of Pope John VIII. The abbey was exempt, answering directly to the pope, from 1124 or perhaps earlier, and its abbots were granted Reichsfreiheit in 1215. The office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogt">Vogt</a> passed from the counts of Oettingen to the counts of Württemberg in 1370.</p><p>In 1460 the abbey became an exempt house of secular canons under a prince-provost, with a chapter of 12 noble canons and 10 vicars. In 1588 and again from 1611 to 1618 about 450 people in Ellwangen were killed in witch-hunts. After the German Mediatisation of 1802 Ellwangen passed to Württemberg and became the seat of Neuwürttemberg; it became a district centre in 1803. The king of Württemberg wanted a Catholic diocese here and in 1812 founded an ordinary and a seminary, but they went to Rottenburg am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> in 1817, and the diocese was formed there in 1821.</p><p>In the First World War Germany ran a prisoner-of-war camp at Ellwangen in 1916 and 1917 for ethnic Polish officers of the Russian Army. In April 1945 American troops occupied the town, and from 1946 to 1951 a displaced persons camp held 3,000 Ukrainian refugees in the former tank school barracks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ellwangen has about 25,000 inhabitants. Its sights are the medieval town centre with its churches, notably Ellwangen Basilica, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> pilgrimage church of Schönenberg and the castle, the last two on hills near the town. It is twinned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Abbiategrasso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ellwangen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He added former imperial cities such as Heilbronn and Reutlingen and Catholic lands like <b>Ellwangen</b>, which made the Protestant state more mixed in religion.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-wurttemberg">Electorate of Württemberg</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ellwangen</b> abbey was founded around 764 by Hariolf, later bishop of Langres, and received Carolingian protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-provostry-of-ellwangen">Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen</a></li></ul></section>
